      <nav class="text-xs text-gray-400 mb-6"><a href="/" class="hover:text-gray-700">Home</a> / <a href="/blog/" class="hover:text-gray-700">Blog</a> / Website Cost Australia</nav>
      <p class="text-xs tracking-widest uppercase text-orange-500 font-medium mb-4">Pricing Guide · 2026 · Australia</p>
      <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-5xl font-light leading-tight tracking-tight mb-6">How much does a website cost in Australia?</h1>
      <p class="text-lg text-gray-500 font-light leading-relaxed mb-4">Updated 8 July 2026. If you've asked three "web designers" and gotten three wildly different numbers, you're not alone. Here's an honest, jargon-free breakdown of what a website actually costs in Australia in 2026 — and what makes the price go up or down.</p>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mb-10">By Aman Singh · Build First Site</p>

      <div class="prose max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed font-light space-y-5">
        <h2 class="text-2xl mt-10 text-gray-900">The short answer</h2>
        <p>For a small-to-medium Australian business, a professional website built by a freelancer or small studio typically costs <strong>between AU$1,500 and AU$8,000</strong>. Where you land depends on how many pages you need, whether you sell online, and how custom the design is.</p>

        <div class="border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden my-8">
          <table class="w-full text-sm">
            <thead class="bg-gray-50 text-gray-500 text-left"><tr><th class="p-4 font-medium">Type of site</th><th class="p-4 font-medium">Typical AU price</th><th class="p-4 font-medium">Timeline</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-100">
              <tr><td class="p-4">Landing page (1 page)</td><td class="p-4 font-medium">AU$1,200–2,000</td><td class="p-4 text-gray-500">~1 week</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-4">Business website (5–8 pages)</td><td class="p-4 font-medium">AU$3,000–6,000</td><td class="p-4 text-gray-500">2–3 weeks</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-4">E-commerce store</td><td class="p-4 font-medium">AU$5,000–12,000</td><td class="p-4 text-gray-500">4–6 weeks</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-4">AI / SaaS web app</td><td class="p-4 font-medium">AU$8,000+</td><td class="p-4 text-gray-500">6–8 weeks+</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h2 class="text-2xl mt-10 text-gray-900">The three ways to get a website (and what each costs)</h2>
        <p><strong>1. DIY builders (Wix, Squarespace, Shopify).</strong> Roughly AU$20–60/month. Cheapest upfront and fine for a very simple presence. The catch: you do all the work, templates look like everyone else's, and they often convert poorly because they're not built around <em>your</em> customer's journey. Great for a hobby, risky for a business that needs leads.</p>
        <p><strong>2. Freelancer or small studio.</strong> AU$1,500–8,000 for a one-off build. The sweet spot for most small businesses — you get a custom, conversion-focused site without agency overhead. Delivery is usually weeks, not months, and you deal directly with the person building it.</p>
        <p><strong>3. Full agency.</strong> AU$10,000–50,000+. You're paying for account managers, strategists, and process. Worth it for larger companies with big budgets; overkill for a local business that just needs a site that brings in enquiries.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl mt-10 text-gray-900">What actually drives the price</h2>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>Number of pages</strong> — a 3-page site is far cheaper than a 15-page one.</li>
          <li><strong>E-commerce &amp; payments</strong> — carts, checkout, inventory and Stripe integration add real work.</li>
          <li><strong>Custom design vs template</strong> — a bespoke look costs more than a themed one, but sets you apart.</li>
          <li><strong>Copywriting &amp; photography</strong> — if you supply the content, you save; if the studio writes it, you pay for it.</li>
          <li><strong>Integrations</strong> — booking systems, CRMs, AI chatbots, email automation all add scope.</li>
          <li><strong>SEO &amp; performance</strong> — a fast, Google-ready build takes more care than a "just make it live" job.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 class="text-2xl mt-10 text-gray-900">What a website costs by business type</h2>
        <p>Price depends heavily on <em>what</em> your business needs the site to do. Here's a realistic 2026 AUD guide by industry:</p>
        <div class="border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden my-8">
          <table class="w-full text-sm">
            <thead class="bg-gray-50 text-gray-500 text-left"><tr><th class="p-4 font-medium">Business type</th><th class="p-4 font-medium">Typical AU cost</th><th class="p-4 font-medium">What drives it</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-100">
              <tr><td class="p-4">Tradie (plumber, electrician, builder)</td><td class="p-4 font-medium">AU$1,500–4,000</td><td class="p-4 text-gray-500">Local SEO, quote form, service-area pages, click-to-call</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-4">Café / restaurant</td><td class="p-4 font-medium">AU$2,500–6,000</td><td class="p-4 text-gray-500">Menu, booking/reservations, photos, opening hours schema</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-4">Professional services (accountant, lawyer, consultant)</td><td class="p-4 font-medium">AU$3,000–7,000</td><td class="p-4 text-gray-500">Multiple service pages, trust signals, lead forms</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-4">Real estate / property</td><td class="p-4 font-medium">AU$5,000–12,000</td><td class="p-4 text-gray-500">Listings, search/filter, CRM integration, IDX feeds</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-4">Retail / e-commerce</td><td class="p-4 font-medium">AU$5,000–15,000</td><td class="p-4 text-gray-500">Cart, Stripe checkout, inventory, shipping, admin</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-4">Startup / SaaS web app</td><td class="p-4 font-medium">AU$8,000+</td><td class="p-4 text-gray-500">Auth, billing, dashboards, custom features, AI</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>A local tradie who mostly needs to be found on Google and take quote requests sits at the lower end. A retailer selling 200 products with live inventory sits much higher because there's simply more to build and test.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl mt-10 text-gray-900">Website cost by Australian city</h2>
        <p>Prices vary a little by city, mostly because of local demand and studio overheads — but a good freelancer or small studio can serve you anywhere in Australia remotely, often for less than a big-city agency.</p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong><a href="/web-design-sydney/" class="text-orange-600 hover:underline">Web design in Sydney</a></strong> — the most competitive market; agencies AU$5,000–25,000, freelancers/studios AU$1,500–8,000.</li>
          <li><strong><a href="/web-design-melbourne/" class="text-orange-600 hover:underline">Web design in Melbourne</a></strong> — similar to Sydney; strong design-led studio scene, AU$2,000–20,000.</li>
          <li><strong>Web design in Brisbane</strong> — slightly lower overheads; AU$1,500–12,000 for most small businesses.</li>
          <li><strong>Web design in Perth &amp; Adelaide</strong> — smaller markets, competitive freelancer pricing from AU$1,200.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The takeaway: your <em>city</em> matters less than <em>who</em> you hire. A remote-first studio removes the big-city premium without sacrificing quality.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl mt-10 text-gray-900">WordPress vs custom-coded — which costs more?</h2>
        <p>This is the fork in the road that decides a lot of your price and your long-term costs.</p>
        <p><strong>WordPress / page builders (Elementor, Divi).</strong> Cheaper to start (AU$1,500–5,000) and easy to find someone to build one. The trade-offs: plugins that need constant updating, slower performance, security patching, and ongoing hosting that suits WordPress (AU$20–50/month). Great for content-heavy sites and blogs; can get bloated fast.</p>
        <p><strong>Custom-coded (React / Next.js).</strong> Sometimes a little more upfront, but you get a faster site, top PageSpeed scores, better SEO, near-zero hosting cost on Vercel/Netlify, and no plugin soup to maintain. Best when speed, conversions, and low running costs matter. This is what we build.</p>
        <p>Rule of thumb: if you'll edit content daily yourself, WordPress can make sense. If you want a fast, low-maintenance site that just brings in leads, a custom build usually wins on <em>total</em> cost over 2–3 years.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl mt-10 text-gray-900">What you actually get at each price point</h2>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>Under AU$1,000</strong> — DIY builder or a template job. Fine for a placeholder; rarely converts or ranks well.</li>
          <li><strong>AU$1,200–2,000</strong> — a proper single landing page: custom design, mobile-first, one clear goal, contact form, basic SEO.</li>
          <li><strong>AU$3,000–6,000</strong> — a full 5–8 page business site: multiple services, copywriting help, local SEO, schema, analytics, fast performance.</li>
          <li><strong>AU$6,000–12,000</strong> — e-commerce or a site with integrations (bookings, CRM, payments), more pages, more testing.</li>
          <li><strong>AU$12,000+</strong> — custom web apps, large catalogues, or full agency process with strategy and account management.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 class="text-2xl mt-10 text-gray-900">How to keep your website cost down (without cutting corners)</h2>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>Supply your own content</strong> — having your copy, logo and photos ready can save AU$500–2,000 in writing/photography.</li>
          <li><strong>Start lean, add later</strong> — launch a tight 3–5 page site now; add pages and features once it's earning.</li>
          <li><strong>Choose fixed-price, not hourly</strong> — you avoid scope-creep surprises and know the number upfront.</li>
          <li><strong>Pick low-maintenance tech</strong> — a custom static build can host for free, saving AU$300–600/year vs heavy WordPress hosting.</li>
          <li><strong>Own your code</strong> — insist on the full repo so you're never held hostage by a monthly platform fee.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 class="text-2xl mt-10 text-gray-900">The costs most guides don't mention</h2>
        <p>Sticker price isn't the whole story. Budget for a <strong>domain</strong> (~AU$15–30/year) and <strong>hosting</strong>. Here's the good news: modern React/Next.js sites deployed on Vercel or Netlify can host <strong>free at small scale</strong>, so your ongoing cost can be under AU$50/year plus any updates you request later. Avoid builders that lock your site (and your content) behind a monthly subscription you can never leave.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl mt-10 text-gray-900">A real example: what an AU$4,000 website includes</h2>
        <p>Numbers are easier to trust with an example. Here's what a typical AU$4,000 small-business website actually covers when it's done properly:</p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>Discovery &amp; planning</strong> — a call to map your goals, customers, and the pages you need.</li>
          <li><strong>Custom design</strong> — a unique look built around your brand, not a stock template every other business uses.</li>
          <li><strong>5–7 pages</strong> — home, services, about, contact, plus 1–2 service or location pages.</li>
          <li><strong>Mobile-first build</strong> — tested on real phones, since most of your traffic is mobile.</li>
          <li><strong>Speed &amp; SEO foundations</strong> — fast load, clean structure, titles, meta, schema, sitemap — so Google can find and rank you.</li>
          <li><strong>Lead capture</strong> — a contact form that actually emails you, plus click-to-call and a clear call-to-action on every screen.</li>
          <li><strong>Analytics</strong> — Google Analytics so you can see what's working.</li>
          <li><strong>Launch + 30 days support</strong> — deployed live, plus a month of free tweaks.</li>
          <li><strong>Full code ownership</strong> — you get the repo. No lock-in, ever.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>That's the difference between a AU$4,000 site and a AU$400 one: the cheap version is a template someone filled in; the AU$4,000 version is a lead-generating asset built to convert and rank. For a business, the site usually pays for itself with a handful of new enquiries.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl mt-10 text-gray-900">Should you pay one-off or monthly?</h2>
        <p>You'll see two models advertised, and the difference matters more than the sticker price.</p>
        <p><strong>One-off (recommended for most).</strong> You pay once (often 50% upfront, 50% on launch), own the site outright, and only pay small ongoing costs for a domain and hosting. Best value long-term, and you're never trapped.</p>
        <p><strong>Monthly "website plans" (AU$50–200/month).</strong> Low upfront cost and someone maintains it for you — but you often <em>never own the site</em>. Stop paying and it can disappear. Over 3 years, AU$100/month is AU$3,600 — often more than a one-off custom build would have cost, with nothing to show for it at the end.</p>
        <p>Monthly can suit a business that wants zero involvement and constant changes. For everyone else, a one-off build you own is the smarter buy. Always ask: <em>"If I stop paying, do I keep my website and my domain?"</em></p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl mt-10 text-gray-900">Red flags when comparing quotes</h2>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>Hourly billing with no cap</strong> — scope creep can double your bill. Prefer a fixed quote.</li>
          <li><strong>"You'll own it"… except you don't</strong> — make sure you get the full code/repo, not a locked platform.</li>
          <li><strong>No mention of mobile, speed, or SEO</strong> — over half of Australian traffic is mobile; if it's not in the quote, it's not being done.</li>
          <li><strong>No live preview</strong> — you should see progress on a staging link, not just at the end.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 class="text-2xl mt-10 text-gray-900">How we price it at Build First Site</h2>
        <p>We quote <strong>fixed prices</strong> — you know the number before any work starts. Landing pages from AU$1,200, business websites from AU$3,800, e-commerce from AU$5,000. Every build includes responsive design, SEO basics, 30 days of free support, and <strong>full code ownership</strong> — no lock-in, ever. You also get a live staging link so you can watch progress in real time. Based in Australia, we work with businesses in <a href="/web-design-sydney/" class="text-orange-600 hover:underline">Sydney</a>, <a href="/web-design-melbourne/" class="text-orange-600 hover:underline">Melbourne</a> and across the country.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl mt-12 text-gray-900">Common questions about website costs in Australia</h2>
        <div class="space-y-6 mt-4">
          <div><p class="text-gray-900 font-medium">How much does a small business website cost in Australia?</p><p>Most small business websites cost between AU$1,500 and AU$8,000 with a freelancer or small studio. A simple landing page starts around AU$1,200, a full multi-page business site is typically AU$3,000–6,000, and e-commerce runs AU$5,000+.</p></div>
          <div><p class="text-gray-900 font-medium">How much does website hosting cost in Australia?</p><p>Hosting ranges from free to about AU$50/month. Shared hosting is roughly AU$5–20/month, managed WordPress AU$20–50/month, and modern static/React sites on <a href="https://vercel.com" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" class="text-orange-600 hover:underline">Vercel</a> or Netlify are free at small-business scale — so most businesses spend under AU$100/year including a domain.</p></div>
          <div><p class="text-gray-900 font-medium">How long does it take to build a website?</p><p>A one-page landing site takes about a week, a 5–8 page business website 2–3 weeks, and an e-commerce store 4–6 weeks. The biggest variable is how quickly you supply content and feedback.</p></div>
          <div><p class="text-gray-900 font-medium">Is it cheaper to build a website myself?</p><p>DIY builders like Wix or Squarespace cost roughly AU$20–60/month, so they're cheaper upfront. But you pay in time, and template sites often convert poorly. For a business that relies on the site for leads, a custom build usually pays for itself.</p></div>
          <div><p class="text-gray-900 font-medium">What are the ongoing costs of a website?</p><p>Expect a domain (~AU$15–30/year) and hosting (AU$0–50/month depending on platform). Modern React/Next.js sites can host free at small scale, so ongoing costs can be under AU$50/year plus any updates you request.</p></div>
          <div><p class="text-gray-900 font-medium">Is a WordPress or a custom-coded website cheaper?</p><p>WordPress is cheaper to start but costs more to run (plugins, updates, security, AU$20–50/month hosting). A custom React/Next.js site can cost a little more upfront but is faster, more secure, and can host free — so it often wins on total cost over 2–3 years.</p></div>
          <div><p class="text-gray-900 font-medium">How much does a tradie or small business website cost?</p><p>A tradie site with local SEO, quote form and click-to-call is typically AU$1,500–4,000. A café/restaurant with booking runs AU$2,500–6,000, and professional-services sites AU$3,000–7,000.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>

